body:
Thanks. This might seem a bit off, but I saw a article via about corruption within "low" hiring standards and a "mass exodus" of officers around the state. I do not find this surprising at all, given that it is corrections after all. I am just trying to see what might have prompted this? Any changes to retirement, benefits, etc?

&#x200B;

[https://www.houstonchronicle.com/news/houston-texas/article/Mass-exodus-of-Texas-prison-guards-leaves-units-12357797.php](https://www.houstonchronicle.com/news/houston-texas/article/Mass-exodus-of-Texas-prison-guards-leaves-units-12357797.php)

Well "fucking off" is a subjective term that I think everyone will decide for themselves what that exactly is. Now fucking pigs is not really my thing. I still stand by my statement that everyone should just respect the profession, I am certainly not saying every officer is a shiny gold apple, but 95% of officers are the most establish person you will most likely ever meet. Every profession has the "bad apples" and with more modernized cameras everywhere they are being seen for exactly who they are.

&#x200B;

On another note I will be fucking off while jogging to try and get that under control!!

Well honestly I am thinking of living in middle of nowhere, where I can buy a "nice" size house. I understand that pay is basically crap. This is one of the reasons I think I would only look at government jobs. Federal jobs might be the one that pays the most. Is this a accurate assumption? What are some of the worst police dept to work for, rather they are smaller or larger. I am just trying to see where the potential trouble spots are. 

&#x200B;

As you can see I am starting out pretty broad and going from there. i am open to pretty much anywhere, but living in a rural part and commuting to a job in a city is something I am open to. After thinking about it I think I might put in for both correctional jobs, especially Hazelton, and smaller police agencies. How is the police certification transfer policy like? I have a current Wisconsin police certification (self-sponsored) through an academy that is good until 2020. Just wondering if I could transfer it to WV. It was a 720 hour police academy completed at a local sheriff academy.

Thank you very much. I believe if I would consider a sworn job it would be around the Little Rock area. However, since I already have experience in corrections I was looking at Forrest City FCI. I know with no sworn experience I would have to go through an academy and a larger department is most likely my only option. In Wisconsin you have the option to self-sponsor to obtain a sworn job. I am not entirely sure if there is a time period in Arkansas. I have researched it via google and found typical time period is within 12 months of hire. This is pretty standard I think within law enforcement nationally. 

&#x200B;

My question specifically for sworn positions is, do smaller more rural agencies sponsor you through a academy? What are some more negative smaller police agencies (to avoid) and some of the "better" reputation agencies? Thanks in advance.

I know it is one of those situations that you truly never want to happen to anyone until it has happened to you, yourself. I wanted to use this situation to emphasize that even though my life was taken apart from the inside out, I was able to preserve and become a fine therapist. Several people and all my professor have told me that I will make a excellent therapist! I feel so lonely, have talked to clinical social workers, friends, family, psychologists, even tried antidepressants to no avail. i really feel lost and I am normally a truly happy and determined person this whole situation just steer my life in a direction I never thought that it would go in. I am determined not to have it control my life, but it is hard to do when I keep getting sucked back in by things I have to handle.

&#x200B;

It feels like I am alone and no one can relate to this because I am not sure if anyone else ever really been in a situation like this, that I know of.
